{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"06","section":"04","chapter_title":"GitHub Pages Personal Website","title":"Publishing With GitHub Pages","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting publishing with github pages to GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website","cat100_supplement_publishing_with_github_pages","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/06/03"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_6_4_publishing_with_github_pages_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_6_4_publishing_with_github_pages_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_6_4_publishing_with_github_pages_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_6_4_publishing_with_github_pages_calibration"]},"content":"# Publishing With GitHub Pages\n\n![Publishing With GitHub Pages textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/06-04-publishing-with-github-pages.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nPublishing is the moment your files stop being a local experiment and become a live URL anyone can open. GitHub Pages does this by taking a branch of your repo, running a build, and serving the result over HTTPS for free. The catch that trips up nearly every first-time publisher is that the live site is not the same environment as your editor preview: paths resolve differently, the build takes a minute, and a project site lives under a subpath. A page that works perfectly on your laptop can appear unstyled or broken the instant it is live.\n\nThis section teaches you to enable Pages, understand why local and live differ, and verify on the real URL the way a visitor would. The single most valuable habit here is testing the published address, not the preview, because that is where the truth lives.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student pushes his finished portfolio, enables Pages, and opens the live link. The text is all there but completely unstyled: black on white, default fonts, no layout. He is sure the stylesheet works because it looks perfect in his editor's preview. He spends an hour rewriting CSS that was never broken. The real cause is one character in the link tag: his stylesheet path starts with a slash, `/style.css`, which means \"from the domain root.\" On a project site served from `username.github.io/portfolio/`, the domain root has no `style.css`, so the browser silently gets a 404 and renders the page with no styles.\n\nThe bug was invisible in the editor preview, which serves from the folder root, and visible only on the live subpath. This section is about closing that gap between what you see locally and what visitors actually get.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nThree facts about GitHub Pages explain almost every publishing surprise.\n\n**Pages is a build-and-serve pipeline, not a live file mirror.** When you push, GitHub queues a build and then serves the output. There is a delay (often under a minute, sometimes longer), and a recent change is not live until the build finishes. \"I pushed and nothing changed\" is usually \"the build has not completed and your browser is showing a cached copy.\"\n\n**Absolute versus relative paths.** A path beginning with `/` is absolute: it starts from the domain root, `username.github.io/`. A path with no leading slash is relative: it starts from the current page's folder. On a user site served from the root, absolute paths happen to work. On a project site served from `/portfolio/`, an absolute `/style.css` points outside your project and fails, while a relative `assets/style.css` resolves correctly. This single distinction causes the unstyled-page case.\n\n**The live URL is the source of truth.** Your editor preview, a local server, and the published site are three different environments. Caching, build timing, and the subpath all mean the only reliable test is opening the actual published address, ideally in a private window (to dodge cache) and on a phone (to catch layout problems).\n\nThese facts connect to the course anchors. NACE career readiness frames publishing as shipping: professionals are judged on what is live, not on what works locally. data-ink and visual evidence applies to verification itself; the strongest evidence that your fix worked is a screenshot or check of the live URL, not a claim that it looked fine in the editor.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Your site is a project site served from username.github.io/portfolio/. Your stylesheet link reads <link rel='stylesheet' href='/style.css'>. What happens to the styles on the live URL?\" explanation=\"A leading slash makes the path absolute, resolving from the domain root username.github.io/style.css, which does not exist on a project subpath. The browser silently gets a 404 and renders unstyled. A relative path like assets/style.css resolves under /portfolio/ and loads correctly.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Styles fail: the absolute path resolves to the domain root, which has no style.css on a project subpath\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Styles load fine: a leading slash always points at the current folder\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Styles load only after the CSS file is rewritten; the path is irrelevant\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nMaria pushed her files but the live page shows unstyled text. Follow the publishing checklist that finds and fixes the break.\n\n**Step 1. Turn Pages on.** In the repo: Settings, Pages, Source, Deploy from a branch, Branch: main, Folder: / (root), Save. GitHub reports: Your site is live at https://mlopez.github.io/portfolio/.\n\n**Step 2. Reproduce the bug on the live URL, not the editor.** Locally the page is styled. On the live project URL the CSS is missing. The difference is the subpath /portfolio/.\n\n**Step 3. Inspect the link tag.**\n\n```html\n<!-- before: absolute path points at the domain root, 404s on a project site -->\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"/style.css\">\n\n<!-- after: relative path resolves under /portfolio/ -->\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"assets/style.css\">\n```\n\n**Step 4. Verify like a visitor.** Commit, wait for the Pages build to finish, then hard-refresh and open the live URL in a private window on a phone. Styles now load.\n\nThe fix was one character of path logic, not a broken deploy. The lesson is that a project site serves from a subpath, so absolute paths that work locally break online. Verifying on the live URL, not the editor preview, is what exposed it.\n\nOrder the publishing-and-verify steps so you diagnose before you rewrite. Enabling Pages and waiting for the build come first, and confirming on the real URL comes last.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Enable Pages: Settings, Pages, Deploy from a branch, Branch main, Save\",\"Wait for the Pages build to finish before judging the result\",\"Reproduce the problem on the live URL, not the editor preview\",\"Inspect the link tag and switch absolute /style.css to relative assets/style.css\",\"Commit and push the fix, then wait for the rebuild\",\"Hard-refresh in a private window on a phone to confirm styles load\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen the live page looks wrong, do not start editing. Diagnose with three checks in order:\n\n1. **Build check:** Did the Pages build finish, and are you looking at a fresh copy? Wait for the build, then hard-refresh or use a private window so caching is not lying to you.\n2. **Path check:** Are your asset links relative (`assets/style.css`) or absolute (`/style.css`)? On a project subpath, absolute paths fail. Match the path style to where the site is actually served.\n3. **Environment check:** Did you verify on the live URL or only in the editor preview? If only the preview, you have not actually tested publishing.\n\nMost \"broken deploy\" panics are really a caching delay or one absolute path. Diagnose before you rewrite.\n\nWalk the unstyled-page case through the three checks and predict each verdict before you reveal it. The page is styled locally but plain on the live project URL at /portfolio/.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Diagnose the unstyled live page with the build, path, and environment checks\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Build check first: the Pages dashboard shows the latest build succeeded and a private-window reload still shows no styles. Is build timing or cache the cause?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes, wait longer and it will fix itself\",\"No, a fresh private-window load with a finished build rules out build and cache\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"A successful build plus a clean private-window load eliminates timing and caching. The cause is elsewhere, so move to the path check.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Path check next: the link tag reads href=/style.css and the site serves from /portfolio/. Will the stylesheet load?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes, the leading slash points into the project folder\",\"No, the absolute path resolves to the domain root, which has no style.css\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The absolute path points to username.github.io/style.css, outside the project, so it 404s. Switch to the relative assets/style.css that resolves under /portfolio/.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Environment check: prove the fix on the live URL, not the editor. Open the published address in a private window on a phone after the rebuild. Verdict: one path change fixed it, found by ruling out build and cache before touching the CSS.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe biggest misreading is trusting the editor preview as proof the published site works. The preview and the live site are different environments; a path or build issue exists only on the live URL. Treating the preview as ground truth is how students rewrite working CSS for an hour.\n\nA second misreading is assuming a push is instant. Pages builds take time, and browsers cache aggressively, so \"nothing changed\" frequently means \"wait and hard-refresh,\" not \"my code is wrong.\"\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the rule to the unstyled-page case. The student first runs the build check: the Pages dashboard shows the latest build succeeded, and a private-window reload still shows no styles, so caching and build timing are ruled out. He runs the path check and finds `<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"/style.css\">`. Because his site is a project site at `/portfolio/`, that absolute path resolves to `username.github.io/style.css`, which does not exist. He changes it to the relative `assets/style.css`, which resolves under `/portfolio/`. He runs the environment check by opening the live URL, not the preview, in a private window on his phone; the styles now load.\n\nThe fix was one path, found by diagnosis rather than guesswork. The walkthrough shows the value of the ordered checks: ruling out build and cache first meant he did not waste time, and the path check pointed straight at the real cause.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader treats the live URL as the experiment and the editor as a hypothesis. As you study the worked example, ask at each step which environment is being tested and what a difference between them would imply. If the page works locally but not live, your attention should go immediately to paths, build state, and cache, in that order, rather than to the CSS itself.\n\nA strong reader of GitHub Pages publishing can answer four questions:\n\n1. Why can a page look perfect in the editor but unstyled on the live URL?\n2. When does an absolute path work and when does it break?\n3. What two browser habits (private window, hard refresh) rule out caching as the cause?\n4. What is the single most reliable way to prove a fix actually shipped?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour published site is strong when you can point to evidence from the live URL, not the preview, that it works for a visitor: the styled page loading in a private window, links resolving, layout holding on a phone. Verification on the real address is the artifact, not just the code change.\n\nA strong submission names the environment difference you resolved: \"Locally my CSS loaded; live it 404ed because I used an absolute path on a project subpath. I switched to a relative path and confirmed the fix on the live URL in a private window.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same publishing logic to a different host, say a Netlify or Cloudflare Pages deploy of the same site. The exact menu to enable the site differs, but the deep facts carry over: there is a build step with a delay, paths must match where the site is served, and the only trustworthy test is the live URL in a clean browser session. A student who learned to diagnose local-versus-live differences on GitHub Pages will diagnose the same class of bug on any static host, because the cause (environment mismatch) is platform-independent. Recognizing that the skill is \"verify the deployed environment,\" not \"click the GitHub Pages button,\" is the sign you understood publishing rather than memorizing one dashboard.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/06-04-publishing-with-github-pages-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"06.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **NACE career readiness**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks for CAT 100 06.04. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to ISTE digital citizenship. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_publishing_with_github_pages\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks records audience, evidence source, AI/software support, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_publishing_with_github_pages\", \"cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website\", \"evidence_based_revision\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- NACE career readiness. https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined\n- ISTE digital citizenship. https://iste.org/standards/students\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- WCAG accessibility. https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_4_publishing_with_github_pages_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_publishing_with_github_pages","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_publishing_with_github_pages","cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website"],"stem":"In repository Settings, under Pages, which Source setting publishes the site directly from your committed files?","options":["Deploy from a branch, then choose main and / (root)","Disable Pages","Source set to None","Upload a ZIP each time you change a file"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Choosing Deploy from a branch with main and the root folder tells GitHub Pages to build the live site from the files already committed on that branch.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_4_pages_source_unnecessary","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_4_publishing_with_github_pages_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_publishing_with_github_pages","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_publishing_with_github_pages"],"stem":"Your page links the stylesheet as <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"/style.css\"> and works locally, but on the published project site at username.github.io/portfolio the styles disappear. What is the fix?","options":["Use a relative path like href=\"assets/style.css\" (or include the /portfolio/ prefix) so the link resolves under the project subpath","Delete the stylesheet","Switch the whole site to a paid host","Rename style.css to style.txt"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A leading-slash path points at the domain root, not the /portfolio subpath, so the CSS 404s online. A relative path resolves correctly under the project site.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_4_root_paths_always_work","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_4_publishing_with_github_pages_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat100_supplement_publishing_with_github_pages"],"stem":"You push a change but the live page still shows the old version. An AI tool says 'the deploy is broken, recreate the repo.' Before doing anything drastic, what is the best check?","options":["Recreate the repo immediately as the AI said","Hard-refresh the browser to clear the cache and check the Actions or Pages build status, since deploys take a minute and browsers cache","Push the same commit ten more times","Email GitHub support to reset your account"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Stale live pages are usually a build delay or a cached browser copy, not a broken repo. Verify the build finished and clear the cache before any destructive AI-suggested fix.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_4_publish_is_instant","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_4_publishing_with_github_pages_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_publishing_with_github_pages","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_publishing_with_github_pages","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"After publishing, what is the strongest evidence that your site actually works for a visitor, not just for you?","options":["It looks fine in your own editor preview","You open the live username.github.io URL in a private window on another device and confirm every link and image loads","The commit succeeded with no error","A friend says the idea sounds good"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Local previews hide path and caching problems. Loading the live URL in a fresh private window on another device tests what a real visitor receives.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_4_local_preview_equals_live","difficulty":"medium"}]}}